   "question": "What have MPs said about reconciliation and recognition of First Nations peoples?",
   "answer": "Based on the provided context, MPs expressed a range of views about reconciliation and the recognition of First Nations peoples:\n\n- Some MPs spoke strongly in favour of reconciliation as a process of acknowledging past wrongs, healing, and moving forward together. One MP said reconciliation requires \u201cacknowledging the wrongs of the past and building a new, inclusive, relationship with Aboriginal Australians.\u201d Another read statements from students emphasising that reconciliation means Aboriginal and non-Aboriginal Australians \u201cworking as one\u201d and accepting one another\u2019s cultural backgrounds.\n\n- Several MPs called for an apology. One MP argued the Prime Minister should come into parliament and say \u201cI'm sorry,\u201d noting ordinary Australians had said sorry during the reconciliation walks. Another MP said the Prime Minister \u201cfailed to apologise\u201d and would be remembered for that failure if he did not change course.\n\n- Government speakers highlighted their commitment to \u201cpractical reconciliation,\u201d including funding for Indigenous health, housing, education, and employment. They also noted that parliament had expressed \u201cdeep and sincere regret\u201d for past injustices, but did not support personal apologies or a treaty.\n\n- There were also statements about the proposed constitutional preamble. Some MPs supported wording that honoured Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as \u201cthe nation's first people\u201d with \u201cdeep kinship to their lands.\u201d Others criticised the wording as historically inaccurate, arguing Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples are \u201cpeoples\u201d (distinct groups) rather than one \u201cpeople,\u201d and that kinship applies between people, not with land.\n\n- MPs also debated the idea of a treaty. Government speakers opposed a treaty, saying it would entrench division and harm reconciliation, while some on the other side supported moving forward with reconciliation and criticised the government\u2019s approach.\n\n- MPs also spoke in support of Reconciliation Place as a symbolic national site to honour Indigenous history and the reconciliation process, while stressing that symbolic gestures must be accompanied by tangible improvements in Indigenous lives.",
